  "tour": {
    "title": "What Does Hesed Actually Mean?",
    "intro": "This tour starts with the limits of English translation, then lets hesed take shape in a story, a covenant-renewal passage, and a repeated song of praise. You’ll hear it described not as a single interchangeable synonym, but as loyal love made dependable and visible in relationship.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:loyal-love-god",
        "startSec": 1565,
        "endSec": 1720,
        "why": "Begin here for the clearest working definition. The speakers compare “mercy,” “kindness,” and hesed in Micah 6:8, then insist that “hesed is not a feeling” but “a visible action that shows your posture in the relationship.” They also connect it to the long view of commitment rather than immediate gratification.",
        "episodeTitle": "The Loyal Love of God",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=e530ba3f-428a-4ef5-828c-34800da4963f",
        "durationSec": 4105,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:a88b1fb7-fd1c-415f-b24c-98322c767992",
        "startSec": 948,
        "endSec": 1125,
        "why": "This puts the definition on human lips and in a real relationship. Naomi’s “May the Lord deal kindly with you” is explained as hesed—“covenant faithfulness,” “longstanding loyalty,” or “steadfast love”—and Ruth’s decision to remain with Naomi is then described as hesed love: loyalty expressed through costly action.",
        "episodeTitle": "Ruth 1-2: God Fills What's Empty",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG9761838334.mp3?updated=1664486106",
        "durationSec": 3645,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:2693",
        "startSec": 2030,
        "endSec": 2203,
        "why": "Now hear the covenant dimension sharpened through Exodus 34. The discussion explains the Hebrew pair chesed ’emet as acts of beneficence, mutuality, and obligations flowing from a legal relationship, concluding that God has “obligated himself” to remain loyal to the covenant—even after the golden calf.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 310: Exodus 33-34",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/02/NakedBible310_Exodus33_34.mp3",
        "durationSec": 4477,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "five-minutes-church-history:podcast:12c17b50-0e41-45ff-a0c5-4dc269d7b4ee",
        "startSec": 210,
        "endSec": 299,
        "why": "End with the Bible’s repeated refrain rather than another definition. Psalm 136 keeps returning to “his steadfast love endures forever,” including God remembering people “in our low estate,” rescuing them, and giving food. It shows hesed as durable faithfulness experienced across creation, rescue, and ordinary provision.",
        "episodeTitle": "Thanksgiving",
        "sourceId": "five-minutes-church-history",
        "source": "5 Minutes in Church History",
        "audioUrl": "https://dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/5minutesinchurchhistory/5MCH20191127.mp3?dest-id=668749",
        "durationSec": 300,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips suggest that hesed is best heard as loyal, generous love that keeps faith within a relationship and becomes visible in what someone does. “Steadfast love” is a strong translation, but the stories show why no one English word captures the whole range: covenant commitment, reliability, mercy, generosity, and concrete care belong together.",
    "totalSeconds": 594
  },
